
But first, why should you listen to me?


# Establishing Credibility

Because I'm amazing. When I was on Google Earth, I learned from the best, and took those lessons to make this rather large codebase, such that volunteers could come in and ...
...
...

Evan's editor/webmaster here. Normally I don't say anything, but I couldn't in good conscious just sit back and let this guy convince you to listen to him.

Please, don't listen to him.

He's the one that added the `funky` keyword, accidentally turned a useful book on memory safety into a demonic grimoire, and has a rule in his server that on Wednesdays any language bashing must be in the form of a haiku. [#favhaiku]

Remember that guy that went yak shaving for his game, and even eleven years later, hasn't even started on it? Yeah, thats him! [# He sometimes refers to himself as a software architect, but what does that even mean? Anyone can call themselves a software architect. It's not a protected term. I could say I'm a software architect, why not.]

He says words like "arcane", "mysterious", and "sorcery" so much in his articles about coding, that I'm pretty sure he thinks [magic methods](https://www.php.net/manual/en/language.oop5.magic.php) are _actually_ magic. [# This guy is so weird, he once waxed poetic about _linear types_ for half an hour.]

He once went certifiably insane because he tried to use rustdoc output (yes, rustdoc output) to make a SWIG-like tool that lets C call directly into Rust code. I can't even... I just... I can't.

If there's anyone you *don't* want to listen to about anything, it's Evan.

Oh, he's coming back. Quick, pretend I was adding commas or updating the RSS feed or something.

...
...
and that's when the council of Mayan pyramid archaeologists deemed me to be worthy of joining the excavation crew as the team's resident software engineer.


